      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "Project Overview",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "b2B",
      "autoResize": true
    },
    {
      "id": "ovBody",
      "type": "text",
      "x": -839.1614521635333,
      "y": 75.63728808938578,
      "width": 700,
      "height": 708,
      "angle": 0,
      "strokeColor": "#1e1e1e",
      "backgroundColor": "transparent",
      "fillStyle": "solid",
      "strokeWidth": 1,
      "strokeStyle": "solid",
      "roughness": 1,
      "opacity": 100,
      "groupIds": [],
      "frameId": null,
      "roundness": null,
      "seed": 1063,
      "version": 604,
      "versionNonce": 467089007,
      "isDeleted": false,
      "boundElements": [],
      "updated": 1787493988574,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "301stablecoin was an R&D project focused on an algorithmic, basket-based\nstablecoin. Two keywords define it:\n\n-> Basket-based: the token wasn't backed by a single asset but by several\nFIAT currencies held together, each with its own weight in the basket.\n-> Algorithmic: the logic that keeps the peg (and, in our case, decides each\ncurrency's weight) is driven by code rather than by a human operator, this\ngranted reproducibility and transparency.\n\nThe thesis was behind the project was to have multiple regional stablecoins\ninstead of a global one. The basket would be scoped to a specific\ngeographic/economic region, in our case the MENA region was the primary focus\nand we planned for ASEAN and LATAM as further candidates. Within a region,\nweaker and more volatile currencies could lean on the stability of stronger\nneighbours, while the stronger economies gained a safer, lower-friction unit to\ntrade with the weaker ones. This should've provided less currency risk, lower\nconversion cost and ideally more cross-border commerce as a result.\n\nThe project had two halves. The first was an off-chain statistical study: a\nPython notebook that ran over the historical data for every currency in the\nbasket, scoring each one across a multiple parameters and over a dynamic time\nwindow. Those scores were then translated into a recommended weight per\ncurrency. The method was adapted from an econometrics paper out of the\nUniversity of Pavia and slightly tuned because MENA currencies used to swing\nmore than the ones the original paper was built around.\n\nThe second half was the smart contract. An admin-only method pushed the\nupdated weights on-chain. From there a set of quadratic incentives did the\nbalancing work: users were rewarded for supplying currencies the basket held\nbelow their target weight and penalised on over-represented ones, nudging on-\nchain reserves toward the desired composition without an operator manually\nrebalancing.",
      "fontSize": 17.7,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "301stablecoin was an R&D project focused on an algorithmic, basket-based stablecoin. Two keywords define it:\n\n-> Basket-based: the token wasn't backed by a single asset but by several FIAT currencies held together, each with its own weight in the basket.\n-> Algorithmic: the logic that keeps the peg (and, in our case, decides each currency's weight) is driven by code rather than by a human operator, this granted reproducibility and transparency.\n\nThe thesis was behind the project was to have multiple regional stablecoins instead of a global one. The basket would be scoped to a specific geographic/economic region, in our case the MENA region was the primary focus and we planned for ASEAN and LATAM as further candidates. Within a region, weaker and more volatile currencies could lean on the stability of stronger neighbours, while the stronger economies gained a safer, lower-friction unit to trade with the weaker ones. This should've provided less currency risk, lower conversion cost and ideally more cross-border commerce as a result.\n\nThe project had two halves. The first was an off-chain statistical study: a Python notebook that ran over the historical data for every currency in the basket, scoring each one across a multiple parameters and over a dynamic time window. Those scores were then translated into a recommended weight per currency. The method was adapted from an econometrics paper out of the University of Pavia and slightly tuned because MENA currencies used to swing more than the ones the original paper was built around.\n\nThe second half was the smart contract. An admin-only method pushed the updated weights on-chain. From there a set of quadratic incentives did the balancing work: users were rewarded for supplying currencies the basket held below their target weight and penalised on over-represented ones, nudging on-chain reserves toward the desired composition without an operator manually rebalancing.",
      "lineHeight": 1.25,
      "index": "b2C",
      "autoResize": false
    },
